WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.830 --> 00:00:17.770
MICROSOFT’S PROFIT MARGINS:
Have you noticed that Microsoft has fallen

00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:19.560
out of the spotlight?

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:24.840
Compared to their tech peers, Microsoft is
rarely on the news and not many people have

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:27.170
a strong opinion about the company.

00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:32.580
In fact, I’d argue that Micorsoft is the
prominent tech company that people don’t

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:33.670
have a strong opinion about.

00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:38.200
With Apple, there’s the whole controversy
about their premium pricing and their anti

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:39.300
user policies.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:43.670
With Facebook and Google, you’ve got the
entire privacy argument.

00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:47.380
With Amazon, you’ve got the bald slave driver
controversy.

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:52.380
And with Netflix, well a lot of people are
happy that they’re getting crushed.

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:56.440
This isn’t to say that we aren’t dependent
on Microsoft though.

00:00:56.440 --> 00:01:01.581
The vast majority of the world still uses
windows and even if you’re a mac user, you

00:01:01.581 --> 00:01:04.250
probably still use microsoft office.

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:09.200
Yet, despite their popularity, we rarely talk
about Microsoft as a company.

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:14.010
I mean, they’re literally not even part
of FAANG and their public interest is virtually

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:16.960
non existent in comparison to FAANG.

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:20.710
Despite this, Microsoft’s business as a
whole is doing better than ever.

00:01:20.710 --> 00:01:27.600
In fact, their gross margins are nearly 70%
and their net margins are nearly 40%.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:33.320
If Apple had these margins, people would be
shredding them to pieces, but because it’s

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:37.430
Microsoft, no one cares or even knows other
than shareholders.

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:42.450
Last year, Microsoft even overtook Apple and
became the world’s most valuable company

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:43.850
for a hot minute.

00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:48.710
If this same thing happened with Amazon instead
of Microsoft, we would’ve seen social media

00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:54.030
erupt with outrage and toxicity, yet no one
cares because it was Microsoft.

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:58.200
Somehow, they’ve essentially become the
VISA of the tech space.

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:02.840
Everyone knows about them and uses them on
a regular basis, but no one cares about them

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:04.720
much more than that.

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:09.370
Honestly, this is probably the ideal situation
given that spotlight the hasn’t exactly

00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:10.830
been forgiving historically.

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:15.970
But, how has the company that essentially
created the modern computer experience been

00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:18.170
able to avoid the spotlight so effectively.

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:21.570
Well, it’s definitely not by accident.

00:02:21.570 --> 00:02:26.569
So, here’s how Microsoft became the world’s
largest background company.

00:02:26.569 --> 00:02:32.970
THE PUBLIC MONOPOLY:
Taking a look back, Microsoft wasn’t always

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:34.200
such a discrete business.

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:39.561
In the 80s and 90s, Microsoft was very much
a consumer business and that’s why we all

00:02:39.561 --> 00:02:41.650
know about them today.

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:45.959
As a front facing company, they faced much
of the same criticism that today’s top tech

00:02:45.959 --> 00:02:47.650
companies are subject to.

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:53.030
Many thought that Microsoft was a gross monopoly
and that Bill Gates was an ruthless businessman.

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:57.730
Bill’s social image at the time could be
compared to that of Mark Zuckerberg’s today.

00:02:57.730 --> 00:02:59.480
But, Bill didn’t really care.

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:05.460
He was the richest man in the world and Microsoft
was more profitable than ever, so he wasn’t

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:08.090
going to let a bit of public criticism get
to his head.

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:13.319
But, maybe, he should’ve paid more attention
to the criticism because pretty soon regulators

00:03:13.319 --> 00:03:14.319
got involved.

00:03:14.319 --> 00:03:20.160
In 1992, the Federal Trade Commision would
began an inquiry into Microsoft’s monopoly.

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:26.490
This quickly escalated into a DOJ investigation,
and in 1994, the DOJ would order that Microsoft

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:31.110
was no longer allowed to package other Microsoft
products with the sale of Windows.

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:35.950
However, one nuance to these decree was that
Microsoft could continue packaging in additional

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:37.810
features with Windows.

00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:42.150
It probably would’ve been in Microsoft’s
best interest to play this safe and avoid

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:43.489
testing the regulators.

00:03:43.489 --> 00:03:45.930
But, Microsoft had different plans.

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:50.970
The internet was starting to explode in popularity,
and it was clear to Microsoft that the internet

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:52.879
was the next big thing.

00:03:52.879 --> 00:03:57.880
So, what better way to capitalize on the internet
than to launch a browser, and this lead to

00:03:57.880 --> 00:04:01.080
the creation of Internet Explorer in 1995.

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:04.520
If Microsoft stopped right there, it wouldn’t
have been that bad.

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:08.739
Sure, they were still pushing the boundaries
of being a computer software monopoly, but

00:04:08.739 --> 00:04:11.950
they weren’t violating any court decrees
right?

00:04:11.950 --> 00:04:16.970
Well, this was true at the beginning, but
this didn’t last long.

00:04:16.970 --> 00:04:20.780
It was clear to Microsoft that if they really
wanted to make Internet Explorer popular,

00:04:20.780 --> 00:04:23.330
they had to package in with Windows.

00:04:23.330 --> 00:04:28.020
So, they would call Internet Explorer a feature
and throw it in with Windows.

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:32.659
As you would guess, regulators weren’t exactly
a fan of this classification.

00:04:32.659 --> 00:04:37.339
Internet Explorer was obviously a product
and Microsoft was simply calling it a feature

00:04:37.339 --> 00:04:39.419
to try to get around the DOJ settlement.

00:04:39.419 --> 00:04:44.520
At first, the regulators turned a blind eye
to this, but Microsoft kept pushing the envelope.

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:49.789
Remember the controversy about Apple slowing
down older iPhones, well, Microsoft had their

00:04:49.789 --> 00:04:51.759
own version of this with Internet Explorer.

00:04:51.759 --> 00:04:56.669
They told users and the court that if the
browser was uninstalled, Windows would run

00:04:56.669 --> 00:04:58.979
slower and have more malfunctions.

00:04:58.979 --> 00:05:05.629
This was obviously completely false, and if
Windows did slow down, it was because Microsoft

00:05:05.629 --> 00:05:11.190
programmed it to slow down, not because Internet
Explorer actually made Windows run better.

00:05:11.190 --> 00:05:15.719
Eventually, regulators had enough, and in
1998, they would put their foot down.

00:05:15.719 --> 00:05:19.889
The DOJ and the Attorny Generals of 20 US
states and the District of Columbia would

00:05:19.889 --> 00:05:25.309
sue Microsoft for “illegally thwarting competition
in order to protect and extend its software

00:05:25.309 --> 00:05:26.309
monopoly.”

00:05:26.309 --> 00:05:33.089
SAVING THE MONOPOLY:
Usually, it takes forever for regulators to

00:05:33.089 --> 00:05:38.069
get involved, but once they’re involved,
you know they mean business.

00:05:38.069 --> 00:05:41.409
Two perfect examples of this were Standard
Oil and AT&amp;T.

00:05:41.409 --> 00:05:46.960
In 1911, Standard Oil was broken up into 34
different companies.

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:50.999
And in 1984, AT&amp;T was split up into 8 different
companies.

00:05:50.999 --> 00:05:56.009
A break up like this would’ve devastated
Microsoft and Bill knew it.

00:05:56.009 --> 00:06:00.759
Fortunately for Microsoft though, Bill had
already been preparing to fight such a lawsuit.

00:06:00.759 --> 00:06:06.169
You see, several months before the lawsuit,
Microsoft had actually bailed out Apple.

00:06:06.169 --> 00:06:09.999
If you’re familiar with Apple’s history,
you know that they were getting destroyed

00:06:09.999 --> 00:06:14.710
in the late 1990s and the company was on the
brink of bankruptcy.

00:06:14.710 --> 00:06:19.089
Even though Microsoft could’ve easily watched
from the sidelines as Apple went bankrupt,

00:06:19.089 --> 00:06:21.729
Bill decided to strike a deal with Steve instead.

00:06:21.729 --> 00:06:26.099
Steve agreed to drop Apple’s copyright lawsuit
against Microsoft and Bill agreed to invest

00:06:26.099 --> 00:06:28.099
$150 million into Apple.

00:06:28.099 --> 00:06:32.999
Through this deal, not only was Bill able
to knock out Apple’s lawsuit against them,

00:06:32.999 --> 00:06:36.849
but he was able to create some plausible deniability
for himself.

00:06:36.849 --> 00:06:41.020
If regulators questioned him about his monopolistic
practices, Bill could point to how he saved

00:06:41.020 --> 00:06:43.909
Apple and how he’s actually pro competition.

00:06:43.909 --> 00:06:49.470
Unfortunately though, regulators didn’t
quite buy Bill’s master plan and on June

00:06:49.470 --> 00:06:54.469
7, 2000, they ordered that Microsoft be broken
up into two separate units.

00:06:54.469 --> 00:06:58.580
One unit would produce operating systems while
the other would produce additional software.

00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:04.559
Honestly, this ruling wasn’t even that bad
given that AT&amp;T was broken up into 8 parts

00:07:04.559 --> 00:07:07.189
and Standard oil was broken up into 34 parts.

00:07:07.189 --> 00:07:11.349
But, that doesn’t mean that Microsoft was
happy with the verdict.

00:07:11.349 --> 00:07:16.379
They weren’t gonna go down without a fight,
so they filed an appeal.

00:07:16.379 --> 00:07:20.909
The appeal was instantly escalated to the
Supreme Court, and the justices would suprisingly

00:07:20.909 --> 00:07:25.389
all side with Microsoft but not for the reason
that you would think.

00:07:25.389 --> 00:07:30.469
The supreme court unanimously agreed that
Microsoft was absolutely a monopoly; however,

00:07:30.469 --> 00:07:35.020
they invalidated the break up order because
the judge who issued the order did not follow

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:36.650
the correct protocols.

00:07:36.650 --> 00:07:40.750
He had failed to hold hearings on the remedy
and he had also been having secret interviews

00:07:40.750 --> 00:07:43.569
with reporters while the case was ongoing.

00:07:43.569 --> 00:07:48.669
The supreme court did not rule out the possibility
of properly issuing another breakup order,

00:07:48.669 --> 00:07:54.129
but after being so publicly humiliated, it’s
no wonder why prosecutors didn’t pursue

00:07:54.129 --> 00:07:55.550
another breakup order.

00:07:55.550 --> 00:07:59.729
Instead, they would order that Microsoft share
their application programming interface with

00:07:59.729 --> 00:08:04.289
third party companies and that a panel be
setup to ensure Microsoft’s compliance.

00:08:04.289 --> 00:08:07.439
Microsoft had basically just won the lottery.

00:08:07.439 --> 00:08:11.680
They were able to avoid a gamechanging breakup
all thanks to a few semantics.

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:16.580
Playing ball as a brash public monopoly had
nearly devastated Microsoft, and Microsoft

00:08:16.580 --> 00:08:18.719
had escaped with the skin of their teeth.

00:08:18.719 --> 00:08:24.209
There was no way that Steve Ballmer or Bill
were gong let this happen to Microsoft again.

00:08:24.209 --> 00:08:28.669
And this marked the beginning of Microsoft's
life in a shell.

00:08:28.669 --> 00:08:33.680
TRANSITION TO B2B:
There was no question that Microsoft needed

00:08:33.680 --> 00:08:38.720
to stop attracting all of this public interest
if they wanted to avoid future lawsuits and

00:08:38.720 --> 00:08:39.720
regulations.

00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:42.970
But, how does such a massive company fade
from the spotlight?

00:08:42.970 --> 00:08:46.620
Well, the answer is switching customers.

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:50.150
Microsoft has always had an extremely lose
policy when it comes to people pirating their

00:08:50.150 --> 00:08:51.150
software.

00:08:51.150 --> 00:08:55.640
In fact, Bill has publicly admitted that Microsoft
would rather you use a pirated version of

00:08:55.640 --> 00:09:26.500
their software than a pirated or paid version
of someone else’s software.

00:09:26.500 --> 00:09:31.330
As you can see, Bill was very much ok with
people pirating their software if it meant

00:09:31.330 --> 00:09:33.440
Microsoft would get more marketshare.

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:37.560
As Bill touched on, the idea was that they
could eventually figure out some sort of method

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:40.470
of monetization whether that be ads or data
collection.

00:09:40.470 --> 00:09:46.790
By the mid 2000s, Windows and Office had basically
reached market saturation, so it was finally

00:09:46.790 --> 00:09:48.060
time to collect.

00:09:48.060 --> 00:09:52.930
But, they couldn’t deploy something as public
as ads or data collection.

00:09:52.930 --> 00:09:56.600
Regulators were literally waiting for the
next moment that Microsoft would trip up so

00:09:56.600 --> 00:09:59.080
that they could persue more severe legal action.

00:09:59.080 --> 00:10:05.500
So, directly monetizing customers was off
the table, but it was time to look at monetization

00:10:05.500 --> 00:10:06.500
differently.

00:10:06.500 --> 00:10:10.810
Instead of trying to monetize the end user
directly, maybe it was smarter to monetize

00:10:10.810 --> 00:10:11.810
businesses.

00:10:11.810 --> 00:10:16.940
They had already gotten people used to and
addicted to Microsoft products, so they could

00:10:16.940 --> 00:10:21.990
force businesses, institutions, and computer
manufacturers to pay for Windows and Office.

00:10:21.990 --> 00:10:26.390
They also went ahead and basically made Windows
free for end users.

00:10:26.390 --> 00:10:29.180
So, no one could really complain.

00:10:29.180 --> 00:10:32.580
Everyday people weren’t directly paying
Microsoft at all.

00:10:32.580 --> 00:10:36.190
And, institutions had better things to do
than complain about Microsoft leveraging their

00:10:36.190 --> 00:10:38.550
monopoly and charging insane markups.

00:10:38.550 --> 00:10:43.500
I gotta say, there was legitimate reason to
complain if they wanted to though.

00:10:43.500 --> 00:10:47.420
Back in 2010, Microsoft literally had gross
margins above 80%.

00:10:47.420 --> 00:10:52.630
Meanwhile, the regulators didn’t really
care because they only care about protecting

00:10:52.630 --> 00:10:54.380
the public not institutions.

00:10:54.380 --> 00:10:58.880
They figure that institutions have the resources
and capital to protect themselves if they

00:10:58.880 --> 00:11:00.240
wanted to.

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:04.560
And given that the public wasn’t being harmed,
regulators turned a blind eye once again.

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:09.480
Not to mention, they had bigger fish to fry
like Facebook, Google, and Amazon who were

00:11:09.480 --> 00:11:11.520
even more obvious monopolies.

00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:15.990
So, Microsoft has been more or less left to
do as the please when it comes to the computer

00:11:15.990 --> 00:11:18.210
OS market.

00:11:18.210 --> 00:11:23.800
THE NEW MICROSOFT:
Overtime, Microsoft has further leaned into

00:11:23.800 --> 00:11:28.260
this B2B model and they’ve started to offer
these businesses other products like servers

00:11:28.260 --> 00:11:29.500
and cloud computing.

00:11:29.500 --> 00:11:34.030
Since then, this side business has grown so
much that it’s actually started to outshine

00:11:34.030 --> 00:11:35.980
Windows and Office.

00:11:35.980 --> 00:11:41.360
In fact, intelligent cloud is now Microsoft’s
largest revenue driver having pulled in $20.9

00:11:41.360 --> 00:11:43.370
billion last quarter.

00:11:43.370 --> 00:11:46.720
This includes all of their server products
and cloud services.

00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:50.740
Second place goes to the productivity and
business processes category which pulled in

00:11:50.740 --> 00:11:52.850
$16.6 billion.

00:11:52.850 --> 00:11:57.230
This includes Microsoft Office, LinkedIn,
and other commercial products.

00:11:57.230 --> 00:12:02.470
And in last place, we have the personal computing
category which pulled in $14.4 billion and

00:12:02.470 --> 00:12:07.290
this is what includes Windows, the XBox, Bing,
and other consumer products.

00:12:07.290 --> 00:12:11.590
This means that Microsoft could completely
ditch the consumer market, and their business

00:12:11.590 --> 00:12:14.500
would only shrink 30% if that.

00:12:14.500 --> 00:12:18.930
In other words, they could ditch the consumer
business and they’d likely still be a trillion

00:12:18.930 --> 00:12:20.260
dollar company.

00:12:20.260 --> 00:12:24.190
And that right there is why Microsoft has
completely avoided the spotlight.

00:12:24.190 --> 00:12:29.220
In the 80s and 90s, Microsoft was one of the
most well known consumer businesses in the

00:12:29.220 --> 00:12:33.450
world, but after nearly being broken up, they
made a 180.

00:12:33.450 --> 00:12:37.620
They pivoted to being an institutional business
which allowed them to maintain their monopoly

00:12:37.620 --> 00:12:40.810
with little public criticism and legal ramifcations.

00:12:40.810 --> 00:12:44.840
And now that they’ve made the switch, they
no longer need or want public attention.

00:12:44.840 --> 00:12:50.040
For them, it’s better if the media keeps
grilling on Facebook and Google while Microsoft

00:12:50.040 --> 00:12:53.360
continues making just as much from the shadows.

00:12:53.360 --> 00:12:56.880
And that's why Microsoft has been avoiding
the spotlight.

00:12:56.880 --> 00:12:59.370
Did you realize what game Microsoft was playing?

00:12:59.370 --> 00:13:00.680
Comment that down below.

